    "available_at": "2026-10-13T13:04:41.694678+00:00",
    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nLeaked Documents Point to HEMI-Powered Dodge Charger Comeback\n17 photos\nPhoto: Dodge\nGearheads have been dreaming of this moment since Dodge pulled the plug on the HEMI and the models that came with it back in 2023. Stellantis is reportedly planning the comeback of the HEMI V8 under the hood of the Dodge Charger. The new-gen Charger is currently sold only as an EV and as an ICE powered by the Hurricane inline-six.\nStellantis is desperately trying to win back muscle car purists. It hasn’t had a proper muscle car since it killed the previous-generation Charger and Challenger, for which former CEO Carlos Tavares is supposedly responsible.\nBut Tavares abruptly resigned in December 2024, making room for car guys to become executives back again. The new Stellantis CEO, Antonio Filosa, has repositioned internal combustion engines at the core of the company’s turnaround strategy.\nFurthermore, soon after Carlos Tavares's departure, Tim Kuniskis was back in the game, following a short-lived retirement, as the head of Dodge. Later on, he was appointed the head of Stellantis's American Brands.\nPhoto: Dodge Kuniskis is the man who orchestrated the revival of the SRT division and the return of the HEMI back into the Ram trucks, after Stellantis had pulled the plug on the TRX and its V8-powered siblings in 2023. Following this move, Ram went from rags to riches within a year.\nGearheads expect him to do the same with the Dodge brand. Ram truck sales in the United States are up 20% year-to-date through September 2026, with 369,379 units sold compared to 307,701 during the same period in 2025.\nThe demand for the 2027 Ram 1500 Rumble Bee has been exceptionally high. The initial allocation was sold out almost instantly. When order banks opened, the entire 2026 calendar-year production allocation sold out in just 90 minutes .\nThe return of the HEMI in the Dodge lineup would most likely have a similar effect on sales figures. In the meantime, the automaker is struggling, with just 8,796 total units sold in the United States through the first nine months of 2026.\nPhoto: Dodge Fueled by the inline-six twin-turbocharged 3.0-liter Hurricane engine, the gas-powered Charger SIXPACK moved 3,446 units in Q3 2026. Only 233 customers went home with a Charger Daytona . That is a devastating drop of 92% from the 2,776 units sold during the same period in 2025.\nDodge advertises the range-topping Charger Daytona as the most powerful muscle car in the world, which it is not. Not even by a long shot. At 670 horsepower (679 metric horsepower), the Charger Daytona Scat Pack is short on power compared to the Ford Mustang Dark Horse SC with its 795 horsepower (806 metric horsepower) and the Mustang GTD with 815 horsepower (826 metric horsepower).\nBesides, the Ford Mustang remained the only V8-powered purebred muscle car on the market, making the rules in a league of its own, after Dodge discontinued the Charger and Challenger and Chevrolet retired the Camaro back in 2023.\nThe EV is no consolation for the HEMI diehards, even though it comes with the Fratzonic Chambered Exhaust, engineered to mimic the growl of the HEMI. \" It sounds like an angry mosquito ,\" purists protested on social media the first time they heard it in a video teaser. Can't blame them…\nPhoto: Dodge But now, Stellantis is getting ready to give them the real deal. Automotive News reports that Stellantis is planning a supercharged V8 engine variant of the Dodge Charger to boost struggling sales. According to internal planning documents, the automaker intends to resurrect the iconic 6.2-liter HEMI for the eighth-generation Charger.\nThe company has already informed suppliers that it intends to build around 12,000 HEMI-powered Chargers per year. Production is scheduled to begin at the end of 2027, with the almighty Charger hitting the market as a 2028 model year. Internal documents indicate that the V8 will be offered with both body configurations, the two-door coupe and the four-door sedan.\nUnlike the current Charger powered by the Hurricane inline-six, operating in an all-wheel-drive setup, the upcoming V8 Charger is being developed as a traditional rear-wheel-drive vehicle, which means that the ZF eight-speed automatic transmission will put the power down through the rear wheels in pure muscle car fashion.\nThe move represents a major course correction for the brand. Dodge has tried to silence consumer interest, but it can no longer ignore it and now has to completely rethink its powertrain strategy. Engineers are currently working to adapt the STLA Large architecture, designed from the beginning to handle multiple powertrains, to fit the large HEMI V8.\nPhoto: Dodge In the meantime, Stellantis is officially treating the project as \" purely speculation ,\" according to a spokesperson from the brand, who refused to comment \" on future product strategy, \" as revealed by Automotive News.\nDespite the official silence, Dodge CEO Matt McAlear previously hinted at the possibility : \" Don't be surprised if it would fit, \" he said. Nobody would be surprised. Just glad that it is finally happening.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ndodge charger Dodge Hemi Hemi V8 dodge charger hemi Tim Kuniskis\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Chris Brown’s New Four-Wheeled Toy Is a Cool Carlex Design G-Wagen",
